WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
Mission 1. Before you build anything, find out what's really going on.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.500
Meet Dana, VP of Operations at Ridgeway Medical Supply. Your new client.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:17.000
She knows what she wants: an AI chatbot. And she says 94% of orders are on time.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Then Renee, who runs support, messages you. She says customers are furious about late orders.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.500
Both can't be fully right. Your first job is to find out which, before writing a single line of code.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:36.500
Lesson one: the ask is not the problem. A chatbot is a solution Dana imagined.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:42.500
Ask: if this worked perfectly, what would be different? Fewer calls.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:48.500
Why do customers call? Because orders are late, and nobody warned them.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:56.000
And what does that cost? Money, and hospitals walking away. That's the real problem.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.500
An FDE wears three hats. The engineer asks: can we actually build this, here?

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:07.000
The consultant asks: is this the right problem, and who has to say yes?

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.000
And the product scout asks: what should we take back to our own product?

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Next, map the people. Who can fund or stop this? Whose day does it change?

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:26.000
And notice who's missing. Boise has been rough since summer, and nobody invited its manager.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.500
Now the trap. Dana's 94% measures when orders leave the warehouse.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:38.500
Customers feel when orders arrive. That number lives in the carrier data, and you'll calculate it.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:45.500
Always ask how a number is calculated. Real data has the same trap; you'll find it in the lab.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:51.000
Then decompose. Step one: clarify the goal, and what success looks like.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.500
Two: who wins, who loses, who decides, and how it's measured today.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:02.000
Three: the inputs. What data exists, who owns it, and how messy it is.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Four: break it into pieces, and tackle the riskiest assumption first.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:13.000
Five: the walking skeleton. The thinnest version that proves value in days.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.500
The classic mistake, in interviews and on the job: jumping to step five first.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:27.500
Your mission: check the numbers, interview Dana, and write a scope she'll sign. Your AI tutor is ready.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.000
See you on the kickoff call.
